Two Gentle Eyes Paused, One Silent Start

The Eastside Town Hotel always felt busy from the outside, but inside, there were moments that passed like gentle winds—soft, unnoticed, yet carrying something quiet and meaningful. It was on such an afternoon that Vishwas first spoke to Pari.

She was sitting alone at the reception desk, completely focused on her documentation work. Her head slightly bent, her long hair falling softly to one side, her posture calm and graceful. People walked around her, but she stayed in her own little world, working with a steady rhythm.

Vishwas had seen her a few times before. He never stopped to talk, never even thought he would. He was a manager, yes, but from a different section—someone who usually kept to himself, someone who avoided starting conversations unless he had to.  

But that day, something inside him told him to walk toward her.

He stood near the counter in his white shirt with thin vertical stripes, his spectacles catching a bit of light. For a moment he waited, building the courage he didn’t know he was searching for.

Then he spoke.

“Hey, hi! Did you join recently? Who is your manager?”

His voice sounded casual, but inside, there was a small nervousness. Not fear—just the unfamiliar feeling of talking to someone who somehow drew his attention without trying.

Pari looked up.  
And her smile… it was warm in a way that made the whole air around them feel softer.

“No, I joined a year back,” she said gently. “I was in another branch earlier. I shifted here recently.”  
Her voice had a calmness that reached him immediately—like a soft reassurance he didn’t expect.

She asked him back, “And you? How long have you been working here?”

They talked for a few minutes—simple things, nothing special on the surface.  
But the way she spoke made him feel at ease.  
Her voice was comforting. Her presence felt honest. And her smile… he didn’t know why, but it felt like something made just for peace.

Vishwas had always noticed her—the way she walked confidently but never loudly, the way her dress always suited her personality, the way she spoke to people with such grace. He also noticed that she didn’t have many friends there. She stayed at her desk, doing her work silently, without trying to fit into any group.

Maybe that was the reason she pulled him toward her—her simplicity, her quiet strength.

Pari, on the other hand, observed him too. She liked the way he carried himself, how he spoke politely, how he had a good reputation among the staff. There was something sincere about him, something steady. She felt something small inside her as well—a tiny feeling she didn’t name, didn’t think about, but it was there.

They laughed lightly at a few small things. Just soft, harmless laughter that made the moment feel natural.

There was no exchange of numbers. No plans.  
Nothing that suggested a story was beginning.

Vishwas walked away thinking it was just a normal conversation… yet he carried her voice with him for the rest of the day. A strange calm stayed with him, something he couldn’t explain.

Pari went back to her work, but a part of her remembered the manager in the striped shirt who came to ask if she was settling well.

Neither of them knew it then,  
but this quiet meeting was the first soft thread of a connection that would change both their lives.
